  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure tracking east into Quebec will lift a warm towards\nthe forecast area late this morning into the afternoon. As a \nresult clouds will thicken and lower from SW to NE with mostly \ncloudy skies across much of the area by this evening. Latest \navailable CAM guidance suggest the warm front will stay south of\nthe area through peak heating limiting the atmosphere's ability\nto destabilize. As stronger winds at 850 mb arrive from the SW \nlate this afternoon, CAMs suggest the warm front make will some \nprogress through SW NH where upwards of 500 J/kg of MU CAPE may \ndevelop. Shower chances will increase from west to east this \nafternoon with thunderstorm chances increasing across SW NH this\nevening. CAMs are bearish on any storms developing robust \nupdrafts while a few storms bringing the potential for gusty \nwinds cannot be ruled out.\n\nThe warm front will make northward progress through tonight with\na moisture rich airmass overspreading the area. PWATs will climb\nto 1.75 to 2.0 inches with portions of southern NH and coastal\nMaine possibly seeing PWATs over 2.0 inches. These high PWATs\ncombined with deep warm cloud depths and pockets of elevated\ninstability will bring potential for torrential downpours. CAMs\nshow swift moving storms capable of rainfall rates of 1-2 inches\nper hour with localized total amounts in excess of 3 inches.\nHowever, CAMs are variable in the location of these higher \namounts and are generally favored south NH/MA border with much \nof the area likely to see 0.5 inches or less. At this time\nsouthern NH into coastal Maine see some potential for isolated\ninstances of flash flooding overnight with the flood risk\nlowering to the north. The time window for locally heavy\nrainfall will be from 8 PM until 4 AM Wednesday morning with\nrain tapering off west to east into daybreak.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThere will be additional chances for thunderstorms Wednesday. \nNew England will remain in the warm sector. The upper- level low\nover Quebec will begin to vertically stack, bringing height \nfalls and cooling temperatures aloft. As such, a bit of a \"cold \npool aloft\" type of setup as thermodynamics show low freezing \nlevels and plentiful 3CAPE. \n\nIt remains to be seen how much instability we can generate; which \nwill be contingent on getting some sunny breaks during the\nmorning hours. Some guidance shows an abundance of morning \nstorms, which could still be strong given the cold pool aloft, \nbut would likely result in lower peak instability. 18Z/0Z hi-res\nguidance is advertising 1000-3000 J/Kg of SBCAPE by early \nafternoon, depending on model of choice. Consensus on dew points\nholding in the upper 60s/low 70s adds confidence in an unstable\nenvironment Wednesday.\n\nExpecting weak 0-1 km shear but strong 0-6 km shear, as the 850 mb \nlow-level jet will have already pushed offshore. Very limited \ndirectional shear is noted.\n\nOne of the biggest uncertainties (and likely limitations) from a \nhigher-end event will be a dry punch aloft, which may serve hostile \nfor updrafts. Combined with a cellular storm mode (owing to a lack \nof strong convergence), likely will see storms posing a damaging \nwind and hail risk, but also the potential for some areas to see \nnext to nothing for various caveats mentioned above. For what it is \nworth, both CSU and CIPS are rather bullish for Wednesday, with a \n15% severe risk highlighted on both products. Current day 3 Marginal \nfrom SPC.\n\nShould generally have near seasonable temperatures this upcoming \nweek. Strong cold front Wednesday night should really dry things out \nwith dew points in the 40s-50s for the remainder of the week and \nrain-free conditions as the cold front clears offshore.",
